{"packet_id":"D006-01","doc_id":"D006","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Measure BB Annual General Adjustment cap","requirement":"A landlord may raise rent annually only by the Rent Board's Annual General Adjustment, now capped at 5%, and may petition for increases only for completed capital improvements; utility charges must be part of base rent unless separately metered in the tenant's name.","key_value":"5% maximum AGA","coverage_conditions":"Units fully covered by the Berkeley Rent Ordinance, including most multifamily properties built before 1980, single-family homes with tenancies that began before 1996, and government-subsidized units registered as fully covered; partially covered units also have a rent ceiling.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":"1980","built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"New construction units are those with a certificate of occupancy issued after 1980; federal or state law may prohibit rent control for some subsidized units."},"exemptions":"Banked (unused) AGAs may still be used to raise rent to the ceiling; units exempt from the Ordinance, including units where the tenant shares kitchen or bath facilities and the landlord lived on the same property at the start of the tenancy.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.76","quoted_span":"The maximum AGA is 5%. This means that, if the formula in the Rent Ordinance results in an AGA that is greater than 5%, the AGA approved by the Board for that year can be no more than 5%.","interaction":"Coverage of some government-subsidized units is limited where Federal law, State law, or administrative regulation prohibits rent control.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D006-01","doc_id":"D006","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Measure BB just cause eviction amendments","requirement":"Covered tenants may be evicted only for just cause. Nonpayment eviction requires rental debt of at least one month's Fair Market Rent; failure to sign a substantially similar lease is no longer cause; landlords must file eviction notices with the Rent Board within three business days.","key_value":"1 month FMR minimum rental debt","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in units fully or partially covered by the Berkeley Rent Ordinance, including newly covered government-subsidized units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":"1980","built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Units exempt from the Rent Ordinance, including units where the tenant shares kitchen or bath facilities with a landlord who lived on the same property at the start of the tenancy.","penalty":"Rent Board may adopt fines for noncompliance with registration requirements, including failure to report new tenancies within fifteen days; no fine system created yet.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.76","quoted_span":"For a landlord to evict a tenant for nonpayment of rent, the tenant must owe an amount of rental debt equal to or greater than one month of the Fair Market Rent (FMR) value for a unit of equivalent size in the metro area Oakland-Fremont, CA HUD Metro FMR as determined by the US Department of Housing and Urban Development for the fiscal year in which the rent is demanded.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
